 as LA 480. See LA 480.","linkedByLetter":false,"sections":[{"crn":"55762","code":"BD","typeCode":"ONL","group":"lecture","status":"unknown","statusText":"","partOfTerm":"1","dateRange":"","meetings":[{"typeName":"Online","typeCode":"ONL","days":[],"start":null,"end":null,"location":"n.a.","instructors":["Deal, B"]}],"restrictions":[],"coRequest":null,"specialApproval":null,"sectionText":"UP 480 55762 is Non-Urban Planning Students."}]},{"id":"UP 494","subject":"UP","number":"494","title":"Special Topics in Planning","creditHours":"1 TO 6 hours","minHours":1,"genEd":[],"degreeAttributes":"","scheduleNote":"","info":"1 to 6 undergraduate hours. 1 to 6 graduate hours. May be repeated to a maximum of 16 hours.","linkedByLetter":false,"sections":[{"crn":"55416","code":"BL","typeCode":"LCD","group":"lecture","status":"unknown","statusText":"","partOfTerm":"1","dateRange":"","meetings":[{"typeName":"Lecture-Discussion","typeCode":"LCD","days":["M"],"start":660,"end":830,"location":"223 Temple Hoyne Buell Hall","instructors":["Lee, B"]}],"restrictions":[],"coRequest":null,"specialApproval":null,"sectionText":"Students will gain hands-on experience analyzing transit demand and supply in urban areas of Illinois and developing policy solutions for transit deserts informed by community input. Core activities include conducting spatial analysis of transit access, reviewing transit policy in depth, and facilitating focus groups with transit desert residents. Students will synthesize their work into a professional report and present their findings to public transit agencies and/or municipal officials. Field visits to selected case study sites are a required component of the course."},{"crn":"67711","code":"DA","typeCode":"ONL","group":"lecture","status":"unknown","statusText":"","partOfTerm":"B","dateRange":"03/16/26-05/06/26","meetings":[{"typeName":"Online","typeCode":"ONL","days":[],"start":null,"end":null,"location":"n.a.","instructors":["Allred, D"]}],"restrictions":[],"coRequest":null,"specialApproval":"Instructor Approval Required","sectionText":"Apply by December 1 at http://go.illinois.edu/urbanindenmark"},{"crn":"51183","code":"KS","typeCode":"LCD","group":"lecture","status":"unknown","statusText":"","partOfTerm":"1","dateRange":"","meetings":[{"typeName":"Lecture-Discussion","typeCode":"LCD","days":["T","R"],"start":750,"end":830,"location":"225 Temple Hoyne Buell Hall","instructors":["Salo, K"]}],"restrictions":[],"coRequest":null,"specialApproval":null,"sectionText":"In this course students explore how a range of regulatory agencies invoke international institutions in the governance of urban conflicts. Specifically, we will study and map how inhabitants of specific cities use international human rights regimes to claim individual and collective claims for urban citizenship and rights to the city. Learning objectives include: understanding the history and current role of international humanitarian institutions in urban governance of cities; identification of key local and trans-local actors claiming citizenship; evaluation of the legitimacy of competing citizenship claims, and; creation of a visually compelling story map argument of a specific case study in a city of the global south."},{"crn":"40363","code":"LON","typeCode":"TRV","group":"trv","status":"unknown","statusText":"","partOfTerm":"A","dateRange":"01/20/26-03/13/26","meetings":[{"typeName":"Travel","typeCode":"TRV","days":[],"start":null,"end":null,"location":"Location Pending","instructors":["LeRoy, B"]}],"restrictions":[],"coRequest":null,"specialApproval":"Instructor Approval Required","sectionText":"."}]},{"id":"UP 504","subject":"UP","number":"504","title":"Urban History and Theory","creditHours":"4 hours","minHours":4,"genEd":[],"degreeAttributes":"","scheduleNote":"","info":"Prerequisite: Graduate standing in Urban Planning or consent of instructor.","linkedByLetter":false,"sections":[{"crn":"74565","code":"UHT","typeCode":"LCD","group":"lecture","status":"unk